<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint Lucy's Day</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Feast of Saint Lucy, St. Lucy's Day</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint Lucy's Day, also called the Feast of Saint Lucy, is a Christian feast day observed on 13 December in commemoration of Lucia of Syracuse, an early-fourth-century virgin martyr under the Diocletianic Persecution. According to legend she brought food to persecuted Christians hiding in the Roman catacombs, wearing a candle-lit wreath on her head to light her way. The feast became a universal feast of the Church in the 6th century. Because its date at one time coincided with the shortest day of the year, it is now widely celebrated as a festival of light, most widely in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and Saint Lucia. The first modern-day Scandinavian celebration was held in 1928 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-lucys-day.jpg" alt="Saint Lucy's Day" width="330" height="261" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Claudia Gründer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lucia-13.12.06.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Saint Lucy's Day</b>, 13 December, commemorates Lucia of Syracuse, an early-fourth-century martyr of the persecution under Diocletian, who according to legend brought food to Christians hiding in the Roman catacombs wearing a candle-lit wreath on her head to light her way.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li><li><blockquote>In Sweden, Finland, Denmark, the Faroe Islands and Norway the tune was given new words for <b>Saint Lucy's Day</b>, in the darkest time of the year, and is sung in several Swedish, Norwegian and Danish versions and a Sámi one.</blockquote><a href="/songs/santa-lucia">Santa Lucia</a></li></ul></section>
